🔹 What is ScrollPosition?

Not core JPA — this is **Spring Data** (3.1+) scrolling API, used with Spring Data JPA.

Spring Data introduced:
- ScrollPosition
- Window<T>

👉 Cursor-style / scroll pagination for large result sets (infinite scroll feeds).

Instead of classic page/offset only, you resume from a position (especially **keyset**).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Why not OFFSET?

OFFSET pagination:

SELECT ... ORDER BY id ASC LIMIT 10 OFFSET 100000

DB still walks many rows → slow on large data, can skip/duplicate if data changes.

Keyset (cursor) idea:

WHERE id > :lastSeenId
ORDER BY id ASC
LIMIT 10

Fast with index; Spring Data keyset ScrollPosition generates this kind of filter from sort keys.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 1. Repository

Stable ORDER BY is required. Common styles:

// Fixed size via First/Top
Window<User> findFirst10ByOrderByIdAsc(ScrollPosition position);

// Or dynamic limit
Window<User> findByOrderByIdAsc(ScrollPosition position, Limit limit);

// Real-world: tie-break with id when sorting by createdAt
Window<User> findFirst10ByOrderByCreatedAtAscIdAsc(ScrollPosition position);

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 2. Service (consume windows)

public Window<User> getUsers(ScrollPosition position) {

    if (position == null) {
        position = ScrollPosition.keyset(); // start of scroll
    }

    return userRepository.findFirst10ByOrderByIdAsc(position);
}

Looping next pages (server-side):

Window<User> window = repo.findFirst10ByOrderByIdAsc(ScrollPosition.keyset());

while (!window.isEmpty()) {
    // process window.getContent()

    if (window.isLast()) {
        break;
    }

    ScrollPosition next = window.positionAt(window.size() - 1);
    window = repo.findFirst10ByOrderByIdAsc(next);
}

Or use WindowIterator to simplify scrolling.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 3. Controller / HTTP cursor (important correction)

There is NO `ScrollPosition.of(String cursor)`.

Real factory methods:
- ScrollPosition.keyset()                         → start (keyset mode)
- ScrollPosition.offset() / offset(long)          → offset mode
- ScrollPosition.of(Map<String, ?>, Direction)    → resume from key values

Example resume (after last row id=50):

ScrollPosition next = ScrollPosition.of(
        Map.of("id", 50L),
        ScrollPosition.Direction.FORWARD
);

For APIs, you usually **encode your own cursor** (e.g. last id / createdAt+id as Base64/JSON),
then rebuild the Map for ScrollPosition.of(...).

Conceptual controller:

@GetMapping("/users")
public Map<String, Object> getUsers(@RequestParam(required = false) Long afterId) {

    ScrollPosition position = (afterId == null)
            ? ScrollPosition.keyset()
            : ScrollPosition.of(Map.of("id", afterId), ScrollPosition.Direction.FORWARD);

    Window<User> window = userService.getUsers(position);

    Long nextAfterId = window.isEmpty()
            ? null
            : window.getContent().get(window.size() - 1).getId();

    return Map.of(
            "data", window.getContent(),
            "hasNext", !window.isLast(),
            "nextAfterId", nextAfterId
    );
}

(Or encode nextAfterId / keyset map as an opaque `cursor` string yourself.)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 4. Types of ScrollPosition

✅ Keyset (recommended for large data)
ScrollPosition.keyset()
→ uses sort-key values (indexed columns)
→ fast, stable for scrolling
→ sort keys should be **non-null**

❌ Offset (same family as LIMIT/OFFSET)
ScrollPosition.offset()
→ fine for small data
→ slow / heavier for deep pages

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 5. Important rules

✅ Must have a **stable ORDER BY** (method name Sort / Sort param)
   findFirst10ByOrderByIdAsc(...)

✅ Prefer indexed columns used in ORDER BY
   id, or createdAt + id

✅ For non-unique sort fields, always add unique tie-breaker (usually id):
   OrderByCreatedAtAscIdAsc

✅ Keyset properties should not be null

✅ Check window.isLast() / emptiness before building “next” position

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 6. Manual cursor vs ScrollPosition

| Approach                    | Complexity | Control              |
| --------------------------- | ---------- | -------------------- |
| Manual (`WHERE id > ?`)     | Medium     | Full SQL control     |
| `ScrollPosition` + `Window` | Easier     | Framework rewrites query from sort keys |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 7. Interview Answer

“ScrollPosition and Window are Spring Data (3.1+) scrolling APIs for JPA repositories.
For large lists / infinite scroll I prefer keyset scrolling: ScrollPosition.keyset() starts,
then Window.positionAt(...) or ScrollPosition.of(keyMap, FORWARD) resumes.
It avoids deep OFFSET and filters with indexed sort keys like id > lastSeen.
Offset scrolling also exists but is weaker for big datasets.”

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Final Summary

- ScrollPosition ≈ resume point for scrolling (cursor-like)
- Prefer ScrollPosition.keyset()
- Return type Window<T>
- Always stable ORDER BY (+ unique tie-break)
- Best for infinite-scroll APIs
- Not: ScrollPosition.of(string) — use Map keys + Direction (or custom cursor encoding)
